Introducing ideas and opinions | Express an opinion with a reason, and distinguish agreement from simply understanding another person's point. Polite questions invite clarification. | “I think the library should open earlier because students arrive before class” links an idea to a reason. | Write one opinion and one question about a school activity. | A useful answer states a view with because and asks for another reason without judging the speaker. | A committee chooses between two club schedules. Members explain needs rather than vote without discussion. | Write a four-line exchange with a proposal, question, reason, and response. | The exchange keeps each turn relevant and shows whether the proposal is accepted or revised.
Descriptive and report texts | A descriptive text presents a particular place or object; a report text explains a general category using organized facts. Both need accurate details and clear sources. | “Our school library has four study tables” describes one place; “Libraries organize materials for readers” is more general. | Classify the two example sentences as description or general report. | The first describes a specific library, while the second states a general function. | Your class prepares a guide to a local public space without using private visitor details. | Write a three-sentence description and one source note for a factual claim. | The description names observable details and gives a source for information not directly observed.
Recount and timeline | A recount follows past events in order and may include a reflection. Use past-tense forms consistently and keep uncertain details out of a factual account. | “We arrived at nine, toured the room, and left at noon” presents a sequence with three completed actions. | Rewrite “We visit the museum yesterday” with the correct past verb. | “We visited the museum yesterday.” The verb and time expression agree. | A team writes a recount of a school exhibition using a dated schedule and photos with permission. | Draft four sentences with start, main event, ending, and reflection. | The timeline matches evidence and reflection is marked as the writer's view.
Narrative and conflict | A narrative develops a problem, actions, and consequences. Character choices should connect the conflict to the resolution; avoid adding an ending unrelated to earlier clues. | A student loses a borrowed book, searches, apologizes, and replaces it; the ending follows from earlier actions. | Identify the turning point in that story. | The apology and decision to take responsibility change how the problem is resolved. | Write a short original narrative about a missed message between friends. | Outline setting, conflict, decision, and outcome in four sentences. | Each stage links logically and dialogue respects both characters.
Analytical exposition | Analytical exposition argues for a position using reasons and evidence. Counterpoints should be represented fairly and answered with relevant information. | An argument for reusable containers can mention less single-use packaging, while admitting cleaning and access need planning. | State one reason and one limitation of reusable containers in school. | They may reduce disposable packaging, but washing facilities and cost can be limitations. | Student leaders propose a change to the school canteen. | Draft a claim, two reasons, and one response to a practical objection. | Reasons connect to the claim; the objection is answered without pretending it does not exist.
Presentations and media literacy | Presentations organize a purpose, key points, evidence, and closing. Images and numbers need source labels; confident delivery does not substitute for verified facts. | A slide saying “visits increased 20%” needs a time period and underlying counts or source. | What two details are missing from the slide claim? | It needs the period compared and the source or counts used to calculate 20%. | Your group presents a proposed reading corner with one chart and one photo. | Write an outline that labels both media items and includes an invitation to ask questions. | The audience can trace evidence, understand the proposal, and raise doubts at the end.
ROWS
),'Communication, Texts, and Media Literacy');
